{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c9e3","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07b60-5dda-7951-9c64-98cc389b396d"},"sequence":844,"recorded_at":1780531400001683,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"bd52028a-acb2-4196-94e2-c06d1bbe961a","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"bd52028a-acb2-4196-94e2-c06d1bbe961a","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"bd52028a-acb2-4196-94e2-c06d1bbe961a"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"b79c7bbd-9a95-48b9-913d-da48acbe6984"},"task_id":"b79c7bbd-9a95-48b9-913d-da48acbe6984","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"b79c7bbd-9a95-48b9-913d-da48acbe6984","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/STORY-260905-3t31e9/worktree/internal/secconftest/doc.go`.\n1|// Package secconftest is the conformance-test instrument for the\n2|// security-primitives-and-conformance-ci story: deterministic fixture\n3|// runners, fake clocks, crash points, protocol fuzz drivers, hostile\n4|// strings, and platform capability skips over the Section 16 production\n5|// gates (internal/secprim).\n6|//\n7|// It is an instrument, not a second authority. Every behavior it covers\n8|// is decided by an existing owner; this package only drives those\n9|// decisions deterministically and proves the drive reaches them:\n10|//\n11|//   - path grammars and bounds stay in internal/scalar;\n12|//   - wire decoding, string measure, and bound checks stay in\n13|//     internal/environ;\n14|//   - argv, environment, redaction, escaping, and containment refusals\n15|//     stay in internal/secprim, whose production code this package must\n16|//     not change;\n17|//   - the pinned specification text stays in internal/specdoc, which\n18|//     this package reads only to derive hostile classes, never to\n19|//     advertise product capabilities.\n20|//\n21|// Normative scope: AX v0.5.0 Sections 16, 19.2-19.5, 20.2, Appendix D.\n22|// Section 19.3 provider suites and Section 19.4 lifecycle cases outside\n23|// Section 16 are not driven here; the per-mechanism tests name that\n24|// bound where it bites.\n25|//\n26|// Stated bounds. The crash Transactor is a test model of a three-phase\n27|// durable commit, not the product journal: it proves the injector and\n28|// the outcome vocabulary work, not that any product recovery path\n29|// works. Finalization is terminal for clean and crashed Commits alike\n30|// (a commit-apply fault already performed the durable write, so\n31|// rollback is forbidden and the bytes survive); a pre-commit Rollback\n32|// forgets the operation so a fresh Prepare restages the recovery\n33|// retry, while Prepare/Commit past finalization name the terminal\n34|// state instead of inheriting ENOENT. The five crash-point names are\n35|// instrument-local phase boundaries, not the SPEC CR-MAT-01..08\n36|// registry: registry-style names are refused as unknown, and a new\n37|// crash site added without an arm is outside this model. Every arm\n38|// the suite sets is consumption-checked through RemainingArmed, so an\n39|// armed point the drive never reaches fails its test instead of\n40|// passing silently; all five points are armed through the Transactor,\n41|// including rollback-enter. The fuzz drivers cover the Section 16\n42|// string and vector gates only; containment races and TOCTOU behavior\n43|// are out of scope for byte-mutating fuzz. The IsEnvName and Redact\n44|// fuzz properties pin the two poles a no-op gate fails (refused\n45|// empty/admitted well-formed name; rewritten secret/passing innocuous\n46|// line); the interior grammar and per-class markers stay covered by\n47|// the hostile corpus tests named on each property. Hostile members\n48|// derive from the specification text plus package-unicode tables and\n49|// internal/scalar predicates, so deleting a secprim gate arm keeps\n50|// the corpus and reddens the gate test instead of shrinking the\n51|// witness with it. Require takes a minimal testing handle so the\n52|// skip and fail arms are driven through the real function with a\n53|// recording fake (both recordVerdict call sites pinned); TestMain\n54|// prints the process-wide Report after every test, including the\n55|// parallel ones, so a real parallel verdict is observable there.\n56|// The per-test skip datum is still the `--- SKIP` lines under\n57|// `go test -v`: on a host where every probe passes no verdict is\n58|// recorded and the final counters honestly read zero.\n59|package secconftest"}}}
